 ## Background
-Zero Order Integration, also known as the rectangle rule or Riemann sum approximation, is one of the earliest and most fundamental methods for discretizing the volume rendering integral [S1, unofficial][S3]. It was widely used in early volume raycasting implementations due to its simplicity [S1, unofficial]. The method assumes that the scalar field (density) and color are constant within each ray segment, effectively performing a zero-order hold reconstruction [S3]. While largely superseded by higher-order methods like pre-integration for quality-critical applications, it remains relevant in neural rendering frameworks (e.g., NeRF) as a baseline or for specific regularization techniques [S2, unofficial].
